@page
@model AboutModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "How it works";
}

<div style="max-width: 62rem">
    <h1 class="page-title">How it works</h1>
    <p class="page-subtitle">
        A small student finance workbench built around three everyday requests: "send me the students with past-due balances",
        "build me a dashboard of tuition payment trends", and "reconcile what the payment vendor sent us".
        @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Model.SourceCodeUrl))
        {
            <span>Source code: <a href="@Model.SourceCodeUrl">@Model.SourceCodeUrl</a></span>
        }
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">The rules built in</h2>
    <div class="rule-callout mb-2">
        <strong>FIFO aging.</strong> Payments and aid pay off the oldest charges first, so whatever is still owed sits on the newest
        charges, and each unpaid piece is aged by its own due date. A late clinical fee and a tuition bill from last spring land in
        different buckets, like a standard AR aging report.
    </div>
    <div class="rule-callout mb-2">
        <strong>VA pending-payment protection (38 U.S.C. 3679(e)).</strong> Chapter 33 and Chapter 31 students whose VA payment
        is pending can't get a late fee or hold because VA is slow. Protection lasts until VA pays or @Model.HoldPolicy.VaProtectionDays days
        after certification, whichever is first, and it only covers what VA is expected to pay. The report flags certifications that
        have aged past the window for follow-up with the School Certifying Official instead of putting them on a hold list.
    </div>
    <div class="rule-callout mb-2">
        <strong>Payment plans aren't delinquency.</strong> Students on an active vendor payment plan show up as "on payment plan",
        not hold-eligible. If the vendor cancels the plan, normal rules apply again.
    </div>
    <div class="rule-callout mb-2">
        <strong>Threshold.</strong> Past-due amounts under @Model.HoldPolicy.MinimumHoldBalance.ToString("C0") get a reminder, not a hold.
        That number is a placeholder and lives in config, not code.
    </div>
    <div class="rule-callout mb-2">
        <strong>Reconciliation never posts anything.</strong> It reports differences. A person resolves each one with a note, and
        that's written to the audit log in the same transaction.
    </div>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">How it's built</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>SQL Server</strong> (Azure SQL in the deployed version). One inline table-valued function, <code>dbo.fn_StudentTermBalance</code>, holds the balance and aging logic, and every report and dashboard reads from it so they always agree.</li>
        <li><strong>Stored procedures</strong> for each fixed report. Dapper calls them from C#.</li>
        <li><strong>ASP.NET Core 8 Razor Pages</strong> (C#) for the UI, Bootstrap and Chart.js for layout and charts.</li>
        <li><strong>Business rules in a separate class library</strong> (hold policy, vendor file parser, payment matcher, SQL guard) with xUnit tests, so the rules can be tested without a database and reused from a desktop tool or a batch job.</li>
        <li><strong>Two database logins.</strong> The app login can read, write reconciliation results and insert audit rows. The reporting login can only SELECT from the <code>rpt</code> views. AI-generated SQL only ever runs as the reporting login.</li>
        <li><strong>Google Gemini</strong> drafts SQL from plain English. It only sees view definitions, never data.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">The data</h2>
    <p>
        About 2,500 synthetic students across five fictional institutions and four terms (Fall 2025 to Fall 2026), generated by a
        Python script with a fixed seed. It includes Pell and Direct Loan disbursements with credit balance refunds, Grad PLUS for
        continuing medical students only (closed to new borrowers after July 1, 2026), VA certifications, military tuition assistance,
        employer payments, vendor payment plans, late registrations, students who pay late or not at all, and withdrawals with
        Return of Title IV adjustments.
    </p>

    <h2 class="h5 mt-4">What I'd do next with real systems</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Swap the synthetic tables for a read replica or nightly extract of the student system (Banner TBRACCD-style account detail, plus aid and VA certification data).</li>
        <li>Push hold-eligible students to the CRM (e.g. Salesforce) as outreach tasks, and pull contact outcomes back into the report.</li>
        <li>Schedule the vendor file pickup over SFTP instead of manual upload, and email the exception summary to the team.</li>
        <li>Single sign-on and role-based access, since this demo is intentionally public.</li>
        <li>Add an R2T4 worksheet check, which needs the actual withdrawal date and payment-period details.</li>
    </ul>
</div>
